description:
src/backend.rs:114-125 — CalendarsBackend::update_object rustdoc reads:

    /// Apply a partial update (patch) to an existing object of type `O`.
    ///
    /// Returns `Some(updated_object)` if the backend modified any properties
    /// beyond what the client requested (RFC 8620 §5.3 server-set field echo),
    /// or `None` if the patch was applied verbatim.

The canonical mail-server's MailBackend::update_object at
crate-jmap-mail-server/src/backend.rs:106-126 carries the same return-shape
description PLUS an explicit caller contract:

    /// **Callers must handle the `Some` case.** When the return value is
    /// `Some(O)`, the handler should serialize the updated object and include
    /// the server-modified fields in the `updated` map of the `/set` response
    /// (RFC 8620 §5.3). Discarding the return value causes server-modified
    /// fields to be silently omitted from the response. Per-request auth
    /// context is available via the `caller` parameter, which the
    /// `register_mail_handlers` closures forward unchanged from
    /// [`jmap_server::Dispatcher::dispatch`].

What goes wrong for the caller: a calendars backend author who reads only
the calendars rustdoc may treat the return as an optimistic side-channel
("only return Some when you have a reason"). They write a real backend that
DOES modify properties beyond the patch (e.g. always-set updated timestamp,
server-generated etag, etc.) but returns None unless explicitly needed by
the handler — because the doc doesn't tell them the handler echoes Some
into the wire response. The result is silently incomplete /set responses
where clients see oldValue persisting even though the server changed it.

This is more than a doc-style nit: it's the difference between a backend
author shipping a correct or an RFC-8620-§5.3-violating implementation
based purely on which sibling's rustdoc they read first.

Fix: mirror the canonical paragraph verbatim (calendars-specific tweaks
only as needed). Same change applies to update_per_user_properties at
backend.rs:148-158 if it can return Some(CalendarEvent) under similar
server-set-field circumstances.
